C++ workload profiles add an executable named `<name>_<profile>` and a correctness test named `<name>_<profile>_runtime`. They retain the ordinary core and its test. The common creation workflow is covered in [Your first C++ project](/docs/first-project/).

## Profiles

| Profile | Starter | Build/runtime requirement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cpu` | `std::thread` partitions an uneven array, joins, and checks every result | CMake Threads |
| `openmp` | Parallel reduction checked against a closed-form result | Compatible OpenMP compiler/runtime |
| `mpi` | All-reduce checked against the actual rank count | MPI C API usable from C++, MPI launcher |
| `cuda` | Bounds-checked vector addition with CUDA error checks and host readback | CUDA toolkit, supported host compiler, NVIDIA GPU for runtime |

Profile selection is independent of the host C++ standard and named modules. For example:

```sh
rivet new cpu_demo --standard 20 --modules false --profile cpu
rivet run --root cpu_demo --target cpu_demo_cpu
rivet test --root cpu_demo
```

In Neovim, `:RivetRunTarget` selects the workload executable. The CPU example uses one to four workers and prints `CPU correctness passed with N workers` on success.

## OpenMP toolchain

The generated build uses `find_package(OpenMP REQUIRED COMPONENTS CXX)` and links `OpenMP::OpenMP_CXX`. The compiler must support OpenMP and locate a compatible runtime; an SDK prefix alone cannot add compiler support. [CMake's OpenMP reference](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FindOpenMP.html) describes discovery variables.

A local preset can select an installation. Merge this entry into `CMakeUserPresets.json` and replace the paths:

```json
{
  "version": 6,
  "configurePresets": [{
    "name": "parallel-debug",
    "inherits": "debug",
    "binaryDir": "${sourceDir}/build/parallel-debug",
    "cacheVariables": {
      "CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER": "/path/to/clang++",
      "CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH": "/path/to/openmp-install"
    }
  }]
}
```

OpenMP Run prints `OpenMP correctness passed; max threads = N`. Its CTest sets `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2` independently of interactive Run settings.

## MPI toolchain and launcher

The build uses `find_package(MPI REQUIRED COMPONENTS CXX)` and links `MPI::MPI_CXX`. `CXX` means the MPI C API usable from C++; it is distinct from the removed MPI C++ bindings. [CMake's MPI reference](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FindMPI.html) documents that distinction and wrapper discovery.

An MPI wrapper can be selected at creation:

```sh
rivet new mpi_demo --standard 20 --modules false --profile mpi \
  --compiler /path/to/mpi/bin/mpicxx
```

A four-rank interactive run uses these `.rivet.local.json` options:

```json
{
  "ranks": 4,
  "launcher": ["/path/to/mpi/bin/mpiexec", "-n", "{ranks}"]
}
```

The launcher must match the MPI installation. Run prints `MPI ranks: 4, reduction: 10` for four ranks. CTest uses its discovered MPI launcher with two ranks and expects reduction 3. Cluster runs need an existing allocation and the site's launcher.

## CUDA standard and architectures

```sh
rivet new vector_demo --standard 23 --modules false --profile cuda \
  --cuda-standard 20 --cuda-architectures '80;90'
```

`--standard` applies to host C++ targets. `--cuda-standard` applies to `.cu` files, accepts 17, 20, or 23, and defaults to 20. The CUDA target sets `CUDA_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES` and disables extensions. Select modes supported by the installed NVCC and host compiler; consult the [NVCC compiler reference](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-compiler-driver-nvcc/index.html).

`--cuda-architectures` defaults to `native`. It also accepts `all`, `all-major`, or semicolon-separated architecture numbers with optional `-real`/`-virtual` suffixes. Explicit architectures are useful for GPU-less build hosts or deployment to different GPUs; the CUDA toolchain must support the selected values. [CMake's architecture reference](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/prop_tgt/CUDA_ARCHITECTURES.html) defines these forms.

Local preset cache variables select `CMAKE_CUDA_COMPILER` and, when needed, `CMAKE_CUDA_HOST_COMPILER`. The latter can differ from `CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER`. Use a fresh build directory when changing compilers.

CUDA Run prints `CUDA correctness passed` after checking every output element. If device discovery fails or reports zero devices, the executable returns 77 and CTest skips it. Compilation and execution need verification on a compatible CUDA host.

## Resource options and test results

| Local option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `jobs` | Build parallelism |
| `threads` | `OMP_NUM_THREADS` for the run |
| `ranks` | Value substituted into an explicit launcher |
| `gpus` | Value available to scheduler/launcher hooks |

These settings do not allocate cluster resources. [Task options](/docs/task-options/) covers launchers, benchmarks, profilers, and scheduler commands.

Profile tests have `runtime` and profile labels, a 60-second timeout, and skip return code 77. CTest can report overall success when a runtime test was skipped; inspect that test's result. Doctor reports toolchain information without executing parallel work. See [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) for verification scope.
